{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>נָבֹל תִּבֹּל.</b> He doubled the expression <b>נָבוֹל</b> and so on, because there are two categories in the meaning of <b>תִּבֹּל</b>: the first is that whatever diminishes in any way from a person's original strength is called fatigue relative to the prior state, but in any case he is still able to do what he previously did with a little effort. And the second is the fatigue felt in every respect, which is the category that prevents a man from doing his desire, because no strength remains in him. Therefore he said to him that the fatigue that will come upon him is not a small measure, but rather a measure that according to truth may be called <b>תִּבֹּל</b>, and that is what he concluded by saying <b>לֹא תוּכַל עֲשׂוֹהוּ לְבַדֶּךְ</b>. Or perhaps he doubled it corresponding to what he said ״גַּם אַתָּה גַַם״ and so on." "<b>גַּם אַתָּה גַּם הָעָם הַזֶּה.</b> The reason for his saying <b>גַּם</b> in the first division and in the second: he intends — not only those besides you in this situation, but <b>גַּם אַתָּה</b>, for your strength is great; and his saying <b>גַּם הָעָם הַזֶּה</b> means Israel, because they need to wait, this one for that one, in judgment, as recounted in the matter ״וַיַּעֲמֹד״ and so on, and he said the word <b>גַּם</b> because their exertion is not gathered in a single individual." "And our Sages of blessed memory (Mekhilta) said: ״גַַם״ — this is Aaron; ״הָעָם״ — these are the seventy elders. And one needs to know the reason our Sages of blessed memory interpreted so, for if it were on account of the ״גַַם״ they should have interpreted as we interpreted. However, since they saw that in his counsel he said to appoint a great number from Israel — officers of thousands and so on — which amounts to a count of eighty thousand, he revealed his mind that the seventy elders would not suffice. And according to their words of blessed memory, his saying <b>גַַם</b> is interpreted: besides the elders; and <b>וְגַַם</b> a second time is interpreted: besides Moses — and there is an inclusion in each one: Moses by virtue of his excellence, and the elders by virtue of their being many. Or it is possible that in his saying <b>גַַם אַתָּה</b> he intended to include the people who will experience fatigue, that men will reach when they must wait, this one for that one, in judgment; and he said גַַם a second time to include also that he himself will join with the elders."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:47.211591254Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}